TITLE: SATCORP Motorcade Implementation Guide for ANU Route (/anu)
DATA ENTRY:
Overview & Architecture
 * The Motorcade project replaces the presentation layer of the ANU route (/anu) with a continuous ride through a single district while keeping the existing content, SEO surface, and ecosystem chrome intact.
 * The tech stack leverages Next.js, React, React Three Fiber (R3F), Drei, Postprocessing, Three.js, GSAP, and Zustand.
 * The DOM version ships first as the definitive source of truth and an independent fallback; the 3D canvas sits behind it at position fixed with a z-index of 0, while the grain overlay sits at z-index 60.
 * Lenis scroll smoothing is explicitly excluded to keep the native scrollbar, keyboard paging, and find-in-page honest, avoiding lagging double-damping layers.
The Contract & Theme Adjustments
 * ANU's existing contract values are adopted directly, including a static camera language, a 32-degree vertical FOV, an exposure of 0.9, a fog density of 0.09, and a tempo of 1.4.
 * A single contract amendment appends 'halation' to the post-processing array (alongside 'dof' and 'vignette') to generate proper bloom around the glass and streetlights.
Ride Mapping & Beats
 * The page is re-sequenced into seven contiguous beats (01 to 07): Departure, The Thesis, Truth Extraction, The Model, Oversight, The Catalogue, and Arrival.
 * Beat 03 ("Truth Extraction") serves as the sole hold in the ride, stopping at a red light where the outside world goes entirely still except for the rain.
 * Content is delivered via handed objects across the seat at each stop: a sealed envelope, a ledger in oxblood ink, a tablet/reel, a handset, an annotated map, a broadcast feed, and the final intake form card.
Technical Implementation Details
 * Progress Store: Scroll writes a normalized progress value via GSAP ScrollTrigger without scrubbing, pinning, or hijack, ensuring React never re-renders on scroll.
 * Camera Rig: Bolted to the near-side rear seat, the camera remains stationary while the district translates past it, featuring a damped look-around (yaw ±14°, pitch ±7°) and a suspension float of 1.4cm at 0.6Hz.
 * Lighting & Sweep: Almost all geometry is static and baked, powered dynamically by a single moving key spotlight (the sweep) passing every ~1.9 seconds multiplied by tempo, occasional oncoming headlamps, a slow district color fill, and a constant weak practical light.
 * Capability Gating & Mobile: A capability gate checks for WebGL2, hardware concurrency >= 4, device memory, and reduced motion preferences before mounting. On mobile and narrow viewports, the heavy interior model is dropped entirely, leaving only the window plane, rain shader, and sweep.
Safety, Access & Build Phases
 * Reduced motion preferences completely disable the 3D scene to prevent vestibular triggers, defaulting directly to the fully readable DOM page.
 * A persistent on-screen brake control allows users to freeze the scene into a static lit tableau at any time.
 * The build order is strictly sequenced to fail cheaply: (1) Flat route DOM re-sequence, (2) Stand up the renderer, (3) Grey-box the ride, (4) Light and glass, (5) Dress the cabin, and (6) Arrival and polish.
